A. Where the owner of a plot of land consisting of one or more adjacent LOTS of record at the time of the enactment of the ordinance from which this article is derived does not own sufficient CONTIGUOUS land to enable him/her to conform to the minimum LOT size requirements of this article; or if the topography, physical shape, or other unique features of such LOTS of record, prevent reasonable compliance with the SETBACK if used as a BUILDING site upon approval of the board of commissioners after recommendation by the PLANNING COMMISSION; the YARD and other space requirements of the zoning DISTRICT in which the property is located may be reduced by the smallest amount that will permit reasonable use of the property as a BUILDING site. Such reduction of these space requirements shall constitute a variance. However, in no case shall the board of commissioners permit any LOT in a residential zoning DISTRICT to be used as a BUILDING site which is less than 75 percent of the Zoning DISTRICT's minimum area and YARD requirements as set forth in section 6.03. Further, the board of commissioners may grant variances only upon finding that all of the following conditions exist:
1. There are extraordinary and exceptional conditions pertaining to the particular piece of property in question because of its size, shape or topography;
2. The application of this article to the particular piece of property would create an unnecessary hardship;
3. Relief, if granted, would not cause substantial detriment to the public good or impair the purpose and intent of this article;
4. Such conditions are peculiar to the particular piece of property involved;
5. The special circumstances surrounding the request for a variance are not the result of acts by the APPLICANTS; and
6. The variance is not a request to permit a use of land, BUILDINGS, or STRUCTURES which is not permitted by right or by CONDITIONAL USE PERMIT in the Zoning DISTRICT involved.
B. Public hearings on variances shall be conducted pursuant to section 11.01.04.
C. If the variance request is denied, then the same property may not be considered for a variance for at least six months immediately following the denial of the variance by the board of commissioners.
(Ord. of 2-11-2013, ch. 11.03)

